{"id": "section-body-18077", "title": "Amelobastic Fibroma Clinical Presentation", "chapterId": "chapter-sixteen", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "ORAL AND MAXILLOFACIAL CONDITIONS", "Tumours and Tumour-Like Conditions of Oral Cavity and Facial Region", "Amelobastic Fibroma"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-18077", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Amelobastic Fibroma Clinical Presentation", "runs": [{"text": "Amelobastic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Fibroma Clinical Presentation"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18078", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Slower growing tumor than the simple ameloblastoma and does not infiltrate between bone trabeculae.", "runs": [{"text": "Slower"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "growing"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tumor"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "than"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "simple"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ameloblastoma"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "does"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "not"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "infiltrate"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "between"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bone"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "trabeculae."}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18079", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Occurs during the first two decades of life", "runs": [{"text": "Occurs"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "during"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "first"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "two"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "decades"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "life"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18080", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "75% of ameloblastic fibromas are found in the posterior mandible in the area of a developing tooth. It is benign and expansile, growing as a pushing front rather than invading surrounding tissues", "runs": [{"text": "75%"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ameloblastic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "fibromas"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "found"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "posterior"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mandible"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "area"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "developing"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tooth."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "It"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "benign and expansile, growing as a pushing front rather than invading surrounding tissues"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18082", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Investigations", "runs": [{"text": "Investigations"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-18083", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Radiographically this lesion appears as a uniocular or bilocular radiolucency, most often in the posterior mandible.", "runs": [{"text": "Radiographically"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "this"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lesion"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "appears"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "uniocular"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bilocular"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "radiolucency,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "most"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "often"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "posterior "}, {"text": "mandible."}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18084", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "The radiographic appearance is identical to that of unicystic ameloblastoma, and both lesions should be differential diagnoses because they affect similar age groups and have similar clinical and radiographic appearances. Histologic examination differentiates the two.", "runs": [{"text": "The"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "radiographic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "appearance"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "identical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "unicystic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ameloblastoma,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "both"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "lesions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be differential diagnoses because they affect similar age groups and have similar clinical and radiographic appearances. Histologic examination differentiates the two."}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}, {"id": "body-18085", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Non-Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Non-Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-18086", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Enucleation and curettage and extraction of associated teeth is usually adequate for small tumours. For big tumours conservative resection may be required, where necessary to be followed with reconstruction.", "runs": [{"text": "Enucleation"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "curettage"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "extraction"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "associated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "teeth"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "usually"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "adequate"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "small"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "tumours."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "For"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "big tumours conservative resection may be required, where necessary to be followed with reconstruction."}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1109"}}], "source": {"file": "medical.docx", "chapter": "Oral and maxillofacial conditions", "start": "body-18077", "end": "body-18086"}}